-

While it=E2=80=99s perhaps best known for running AI models on laptops, lla=
ma.cpp can perform inference on hardware that ranges from servers to edge m=
achines. The project supports =E2=80=9Cmore than 150 architecture variants,=
=E2=80=9D the team says.

-

llama.cpp loads models stored in GGUF, a file format designed to load quick=
ly. Hugging Face hosts thousands of models compatible with llama.cpp.

-

The software relies heavily on quantization, which shrinks a model by stori=
ng its weights (the numbers that encode what it learned) at lower precision=
, for instance as 4-bit instead of 32-bit numbers. Quantized models require=
 less memory and often run faster, sometimes at a cost in accuracy.

-

The ggml library interacts with different kinds of hardware through separat=
e backends, modules written for specific chips or programming standards. Th=
ese include CUDA for Nvidia GPUs, Metal for Apple chips, and the cross-vend=
or Vulkan standard, as well as backends for ordinary CPUs.

-

A bundled server accepts requests in formats modeled on OpenAI=E2=80=99s an=
d Anthropic=E2=80=99s APIs, so many apps written for cloud models can use a=
 local model instead. The project makes =E2=80=9Cno strong claims of compat=
ibility=E2=80=9D with OpenAI=E2=80=99s specification but says the server =
=E2=80=9Csuffices to support many apps.=E2=80=9D

