Communicating Agents=E2=80=99 Thoughts

When one AI agent sends a text message to another, the tokens it sends cont=
ain less information than the hidden states of the agent=E2=80=99s LLM. Res=
earchers enabled agents based on the same LLM to transmit hidden states wit=
hout further training.

What=E2=80=99s new: Yanwen Peng and colleagues at the University of Sheffie=
ld developed StateBridge (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC4=
04/VX9XlS2DbV0TW8qTz8v7-Rf_WW8QwHkG5XSKyyN8rPF5s3prCMW7lCdLW6lZ3kWW7xvfVn4y=
HHXxW1RBVxH3lJWJpW6dSw-Y3qBx_FVh4K1J3Gl58FW4QX24G4dyl3ZW5FYqfQ5rRdqlW38yJLt=
77Txj9W28vQyY5PlGhxW4Zk8Bf39ZYM_W2FMntq3KTXDwW8mkTKK1tWdNnW8HdwSR7n5mrwVLBG=
Nw6rWBwqW4H-l8f6-qY_4W5BqNDF2C4RZHW6mtHY_8HrNG9N3VtB9_VztZKN6WF_ym1zcnQW5s9=
-591sVHNKW4c-4rV58hkf1W985rDQ35Wd-VW7wKCDK2BhPV5W3KbFjm3scMxwW75FMHJ5z5rtHf=
6qHQmY04 ) , a method that enables agents that are based on the same LLM to=
 communicate with one another through hidden states without further trainin=
g. The code (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/VX9XlS2DbV=
0TW8qTz8v7-Rf_WW8QwHkG5XSKyyN8rPF5s3prCMW7lCdLW6lZ3lMW5wT_9z7L9P5-W10MXHC6x=
V8ddW2VJGR66lYY8KW7W0j385mxLYFW5lVm0K6ZMslXV2_p7T67Sp4dVtChPB6RFqZ9W3TyFJ95=
Rk7rTW349qWp84yFX9W1S323f4c6b_GTXX706BsXg8W5HFfdR8WYJ5TW24TjVz2sQNcpW6Gtp7X=
43y3T_Vmndsd1ZfBDcW2B28991SgC-FW2cDZq87VgrMdW8yBZ6w5lQThdW45NYTJ6_pb8gW8zRt=
X91J9p2VW4_PjPG5N88TZVWTtd35rgFC_W1Xy3FM5Y8sZMW84Jtkx6y1rJyf8tsbyP04 ) is f=
reely available for both commercial and noncommercial uses under the Apache=
 2.0 license.

Key insight: Each token a sender agent writes has two vectors: (i) the hidd=
en state computed by the final layer of the sender agent's model prior to g=
enerating the token, which holds information such as the agent's confidence=
 in the token and traces of alternatives it did not choose, and (ii) the re=
sulting token embedding. Sending the token embedding alone loses informatio=
n in the hidden state. Sending the hidden state keeps that information, but=
 the receiver cannot read it: The two vectors point in different directions=
 and differ greatly in magnitude, and the receiver was pretrained to take t=
oken embeddings as its input. So the hidden states have to be moved toward =
the receiver=E2=80=99s input space. This is the key to communicating the se=
nder agent=E2=80=99s thoughts directly.

How it works: The researchers built agents based on three Qwen3 models (4, =
8, and 32 billion parameters) and OLMo3-7B-Think. To produce inter-agent co=
mmunications, they used four agents in sequence: planner, critic, refiner, =
judge (roles defined by LatentMAS (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+=
113/cJhC404/VX9XlS2DbV0TW8qTz8v7-Rf_WW8QwHkG5XSKyyN8rPF5s3prCMW7lCdLW6lZ3p1=
VRwJ7J1PBBc9W5FPy_44f7y6-W6G8B-N3YBPbxN5Glr0rWl1XGW6CB_8J7dDxLqW2JxWSk3bWlY=
sW3gQpCn630q9YN8JGFBvzvJtfW7MCR892VG6M_W3N4qnH94nG83W6WtMgF7_yq6BW2y1jNM8LG=
_qgW4hhRfl7TSSnYW795bqT7dJmvxN4Rd9bbXfqWYW289D254JkQ03W5-gTbS8Y_qz-W8ylT9j1=
LrWmLW2xY6LK46BpqkW6lRXdf46CW4DW8zRmCb790VdvW4MxHGb1DM9TCW8r1p_k7vygwJN7pd-=
MbW6QLBf6jL_G204 ) , an earlier method that feeds the sender agent=E2=80=99=
s key-value cache into every layer of the receiver agent=E2=80=99s model). =
Given a prompt, the planner outlines a response, the critic checks the plan=
, the refiner revises the plan, and the judge uses the revised plan to prod=
uce final output. At each handoff, the StateBridge method converts the send=
er=E2=80=99s hidden states into vectors the receiver takes as input in plac=
e of the sender=E2=80=99s generated tokens.

- The authors paired the sender=E2=80=99s hidden states with their token em=
beddings. The sender generated its message, such as the planner=E2=80=99s p=
lan, as text, one token at a time. The authors kept the hidden states of th=
e message=E2=80=99s last 64 tokens, since later hidden states reflect more =
of the preceding text.
- They rotated the hidden states toward their token embeddings. They normal=
ized both sets of vectors, the 64 hidden states, and their 64 token embeddi=
ngs, so both had a mean of zero and equal variance. Then the authors found =
the single rotation that brought the hidden states, on average, as close as=
 possible to their own token embeddings without modifying the distances and=
 angles between the hidden states. Last, they scaled the rotated hidden sta=
tes to the token embeddings=E2=80=99 original mean and variance.
- After the rotation, they gave every hidden state the same magnitude. Matc=
hing the token embeddings=E2=80=99 mean and variance adjusted the hidden st=
ates as a set, not one by one, so individual hidden states could still be l=
arger than token embeddings. In this way, they scaled each hidden state to =
the average magnitude of all the token embeddings in the model=E2=80=99s vo=
cabulary.
- They modified each hidden state to be more similar to its closest token e=
mbedding. For each hidden state, they searched the vocabulary for the token=
 embedding that pointed in the direction most similar to that hidden state=
=E2=80=99s. (It didn=E2=80=99t necessarily correspond to the token the send=
er wrote.) They replaced each hidden state with a weighted average: 70 perc=
ent the hidden state and 30 percent its closest token embedding. This weigh=
ted average moved each hidden state to be more similar to the kinds of inpu=
ts the receiver saw during pretraining.
- They prepended the resulting 64 hidden states to the token embeddings of =
the receiver=E2=80=99s prompt. The receiver then became the sender and gene=
rated the next message. (The judge=E2=80=98s message was the final response=
).

Results: The authors tested StateBridge with the Qwen3 models and OLMo3-7B-=
Think. The tests included answering questions (MedQA), coding (HumanEval+),=
 and math (GSM8K) plus, for the two larger Qwen3 models, further math and s=
cience benchmarks (such as GPQA-Diamond). With every model tested, their me=
thod achieved higher average results than three alternatives (a single agen=
t working alone, the same four agents handing off text, and LatentMAS). It =
outperformed the best of them by 2.4 to 2.9 percentage points.

-

StateBridge achieved its largest margin over the strongest alternative on G=
PQA-Diamond=E2=80=99s graduate-level science questions. With Qwen3-8B, agen=
ts using StateBridge solved 52.5 percent of the questions, compared with 45=
.5 percent using LatentMAS and 39.9 percent using a single agent.

With OLMo3-7B-Think, agents using StateBridge solved an average of 76.7 per=
cent of problems across benchmarks while the strongest alternative, four ag=
ents exchanging text, achieved 73.9 percent.

-

LatentMAS solved 55.1 percent, below a single agent (71.8 percent).

Why it matters: Compared to text communication, StateBridge raised the aver=
age performance of multi-agent systems for every model tested. And the barr=
ier to experimentation is low, since you can jump straight in without needi=
ng to collect training data or train a model.

We=E2=80=99re thinking: StateBridge passes along only the hidden states for=
 the last 64 tokens of each message, however long the message. Yet agents t=
hat used StateBridge achieved higher average scores than agents that handed=
 off the full text. If 64 converted hidden states can stand in for a whole =
message, similar conversions might let agents pass other long inputs, such =
as retrieved documents or tool outputs, as a small number of vectors.

