" data-hs-cos-type=3D"module"><div id=3D"hs_cos_wrapper_module_173703950154=
03_" class=3D"hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_widget hs_cos_wrapper_type_rich=
_text" style=3D"color: inherit; font-size: inherit; line-height: inherit;" =
data-hs-cos-general-type=3D"widget" data-hs-cos-type=3D"rich_text"><h1 styl=
e=3D"margin:0; font-family:Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; line-height:125%; =
font-size:32px; font-weight:bold">Communicating Agents=E2=80=99 Thoughts</h=
1>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">When one AI agent sends a text message to ano=
ther, the tokens it sends contain less information than the hidden states o=
f the agent=E2=80=99s LLM. Researchers enabled agents based on the same LLM=
 to transmit hidden states without further training.<br>&nbsp;<br><span sty=
le=3D"font-weight: bold;">What=E2=80=99s new:</span> Yanwen Peng and collea=
gues at the University of Sheffield developed <a href=3D"https://info.deepl=
earning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/VX9XlS2DbV0TW8qTz8v7-Rf_WW8QwHkG5XSKyyN8r=
PF5s3qgz0W7lCdLW6lZ3p-W6RRpL18--hkSVwKtBp9hT5g3W8qTqMv8JLLw2N9jLYFlC1n_-VfH=
lkf2_BCZxW1JCQnZ4m8j3vW5BHp6w7wlQg2N1j_2TYC5vRNW1ms3SQ4cNNQ2W7T8JfZ83L2PwVf=
CTtc4fmGdnW2sGjv11XrfNKW7tS5Gh1sdjdTW3-nXHn2jfDhmW2vh4CV8jjg5tN1F7_l6S9B2lW=
2ZBbdm57kG86W39H3vf5M6My2W8Kvf7v7hBcjzN2R7s1YfqxN9W4rv2lm8SFxndW4Qh-3N26FZg=
FV6mCDG5Vd8YQW2KG4Z57WK2tqf2_r2gd04" rel=3D"noopener" style=3D"color:#237b9=
4" data-hs-link-id=3D"0" data-hs-link-id-v2=3D"M6UGxThO" target=3D"_blank">=
StateBridge</a>, a method that enables agents that are based on the same LL=
M to communicate with one another through hidden states without further tra=
ining. The <a  href=3D"https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/=
VX9XlS2DbV0TW8qTz8v7-Rf_WW8QwHkG5XSKyyN8rPF5s3qgz0W7lCdLW6lZ3n1W6jBRbX992H9=
kW91xW0M8wzbBlW8zDcNf4L2LKcW27pxFw3Sd0LNW4-dWMm5tyNGgN51_SVvBb80rW3pYjrd1XS=
4fFW1bwb0N7bRHVGW37ZGtG35DBWYW588hzX2LChz_W7s-LGt952qkFW4ZkK-m4CJ0lFW23GqMz=
7g0m4pW86msVz25GGtxW3Lcg2Q1m2SXvW8fF7NT40vJq-W2WmFMX2_QVJNW73YQgc5wzzr4VCs-=
Vj3mbX56W76RlxH5r1xRdW3_sWBF10hZw-W6Z_4_b4rytXJW95rtlF1d8W-0V8Nw654x-D3bf5-=
Kn7604" rel=3D"noopener" style=3D"color:#237b94" data-hs-link-id=3D"0" data=
-hs-link-id-v2=3D"81ibVbcA" target=3D"_blank">code</a> is freely available =
for both commercial and noncommercial uses under the Apache 2.0 license.<br=
>&nbsp;<br><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">Key insight:</span> Each toke=
n a sender agent writes has two vectors: (i) the hidden state computed by t=
he final layer of the sender agent's model prior to generating the token, w=
hich holds information such as the agent's confidence in the token and trac=
es of alternatives it did not choose, and (ii) the resulting token embeddin=
g. Sending the token embedding alone loses information in the hidden state.=
 Sending the hidden state keeps that information, but the receiver cannot r=
ead it: The two vectors point in different directions and differ greatly in=
 magnitude, and the receiver was pretrained to take token embeddings as its=
 input. So the hidden states have to be moved toward the receiver=E2=80=99s=
 input space. This is the key to communicating the sender agent=E2=80=99s t=
houghts directly.<br>&nbsp;<br><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">How it wo=
rks: </span>The researchers built agents based on three Qwen3 models (4, 8,=
 and 32 billion parameters) and OLMo3-7B-Think. To produce inter-agent comm=
unications, they used four agents in sequence: planner, critic, refiner, ju=
dge (roles defined by <a href=3D"https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+11=
3/cJhC404/VX9XlS2DbV0TW8qTz8v7-Rf_WW8QwHkG5XSKyyN8rPF5s3qgz0W7lCdLW6lZ3kBW8=
txYzr5yBT1-W4G3ssv6MX-L8V1JLQ92c2SjdN4kFlJGLZZXPVkm26R9g_n5xW4n1RYh41HTwpW2=
QqS1t2JgD4vW14tYKY8PDPlRW8q_3TX5v-9xwN12qSYM8Y8zsW8hHSrg3KMsKVW7Wfpm12CZS5L=
W69cY1f8S1nV0N8SDkk65WcQvVlNWXz2vBVNpW7T_lyX9fXT86W94CRb_5Zq7N5W61-TvM5LzGM=
nW6C7JjW7vMk_vW32x7cq4dphFPW19vy3s8LHd5sW4xmbT726NqRcW5WMjwq1K4-9ZW1wYSRq2v=
pvSCf9bz3Dg04" rel=3D"noopener" style=3D"color:#237b94" data-hs-link-id=3D"=
0" data-hs-link-id-v2=3D"CzKAhNCR" target=3D"_blank">LatentMAS</a>, an earl=
ier method that feeds the sender agent=E2=80=99s key-value cache into every=
 layer of the receiver agent=E2=80=99s model). Given a prompt, the planner =
outlines a response, the critic checks the plan, the refiner revises the pl=
an, and the judge uses the revised plan to produce final output. At each ha=
ndoff, the StateBridge method converts the sender=E2=80=99s hidden states i=
nto vectors the receiver takes as input in place of the sender=E2=80=99s ge=
nerated tokens.</p>
<ul style=3D"line-height:150%">
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">The authors paired the sender=E2=80=99s hi=
dden states with their token embeddings. The sender generated its message, =
such as the planner=E2=80=99s plan, as text, one token at a time. The autho=
rs kept the hidden states of the message=E2=80=99s last 64 tokens, since la=
ter hidden states reflect more of the preceding text.</li>
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">They rotated the hidden states toward thei=
r token embeddings. They normalized both sets of vectors, the 64 hidden sta=
tes, and their 64 token embeddings, so both had a mean of zero and equal va=
riance. Then the authors found the single rotation that brought the hidden =
states, on average, as close as possible to their own token embeddings with=
out modifying the distances and angles between the hidden states. Last, the=
y scaled the rotated hidden states to the token embeddings=E2=80=99 origina=
l mean and variance.</li>
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">After the rotation, they gave every hidden=
 state the same magnitude. Matching the token embeddings=E2=80=99 mean and =
variance adjusted the hidden states as a set, not one by one, so individual=
 hidden states could still be larger than token embeddings. In this way, th=
ey scaled each hidden state to the average magnitude of all the token embed=
dings in the model=E2=80=99s vocabulary.</li>
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">They modified each hidden state to be more=
 similar to its closest token embedding. For each hidden state, they search=
ed the vocabulary for the token embedding that pointed in the direction mos=
t similar to that hidden state=E2=80=99s. (It didn=E2=80=99t necessarily co=
rrespond to the token the sender wrote.) They replaced each hidden state wi=
th a weighted average: 70 percent the hidden state and 30 percent its close=
st token embedding. This weighted average moved each hidden state to be mor=
e similar to the kinds of inputs the receiver saw during pretraining.</li>
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">They prepended the resulting 64 hidden sta=
tes to the token embeddings of the receiver=E2=80=99s prompt. The receiver =
then became the sender and generated the next message. (The judge=E2=80=98s=
 message was the final response).</li>
</ul>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%"><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">Results: <=
/span>The authors tested StateBridge with the Qwen3 models and OLMo3-7B-Thi=
nk. The tests included answering questions (MedQA), coding (HumanEval+), an=
d math (GSM8K) plus, for the two larger Qwen3 models, further math and scie=
nce benchmarks (such as GPQA-Diamond). With every model tested, their metho=
d achieved higher average results than three alternatives (a single agent w=
orking alone, the same four agents handing off text, and LatentMAS). It out=
performed the best of them by 2.4 to 2.9 percentage points.</p>
<ul style=3D"line-height:150%">
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">StateBridge achieved its largest margin over =
the strongest alternative on GPQA-Diamond=E2=80=99s graduate-level science =
questions. With Qwen3-8B, agents using StateBridge solved 52.5 percent of t=
he questions, compared with 45.5 percent using LatentMAS and 39.9 percent u=
sing a single agent.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">With OLMo3-7B-Think, agents using StateBridge=
 solved an average of 76.7 percent of problems across benchmarks while the =
strongest alternative, four agents exchanging text, achieved 73.9 percent.<=
/p>
</li>
<li style=3D"line-height: 150%;">
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">LatentMAS solved 55.1 percent, below a single=
 agent (71.8 percent).</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">Why it matters: Compared to text communicatio=
n, StateBridge raised the average performance of multi-agent systems for ev=
ery model tested. And the barrier to experimentation is low, since you can =
jump straight in without needing to collect training data or train a model.=
</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">&nbsp;</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">&nbsp;</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%"><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">We=E2=80=
=99re thinking:</span> StateBridge passes along only the hidden states for =
the last 64 tokens of each message, however long the message. Yet agents th=
at used StateBridge achieved higher average scores than agents that handed =
off the full text. If 64 converted hidden states can stand in for a whole m=
essage, similar conversions might let agents pass other long inputs, such a=
s retrieved documents or tool outputs, as a small number of vectors.</p>
<p style=3D"line-height:150%">&nbsp;</p></div></div>
